==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|scrutiny}}, from {{der|en|ML.|scrūtinium||a search, an inquiry}}, from {{der|en|VL.|scrūtor||to search or examine thoroughly}}, of uncertain origin. Possibly from {{der|en|LL.|scrūta||rubbish, broken trash}}; or of {{der|en|gem}} origin, related to {{cog|ang|scrūtnung||examination, investigation, inquiry, search}}, from {{m|ang|scrūtnian}}, {{m|ang|scrūdnian||to examine carefully, scrutinize, consider, investigate}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*skrudōną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*skruþōną||to search, examine}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)krewt-||to cut}}. Compare {{cog|goh|skrodōn}}, {{m|goh|scrutōn}}, {{m|goh|scrutilōn||to research, explore}}, {{cog|goh|scrod||a search, scrutiny}}, {{cog|ang|scrēadian||to shred, cut up, cut off, peel, pare, prune}}. More at {{l|en|shred}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈskɹuː.tɪ.ni/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|scru|ti|ny}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Mélange a trois-scrutiny.wav |Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# Intense [[study]] of someone or something.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|4|passage=Thenceforth I thought thee worth my nearer view / And narrower '''scrutiny'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2011|author=Patrick Spedding|author2=James Lambert|title=Fanny Hill, Lord Fanny, and the Myth of Metonymy|journal=Studies in Philology|volume=108|issue=1|page=109|text=Certainly, upon closer '''scrutiny''', it becomes apparent that current usage rather than eighteenth-century usage is the basis of the interpretation of ''fanny'' as a sexual term.}}
# Thorough [[inspection]] of a situation or a case.
# An [[examination]] of [[catechumen]]s, in the last week of [[Lent]], who were to receive [[baptism]] on [[Easter]] Day.
# A [[ticket]], or little paper [[billet]], on which a [[vote]] is written.
# An examination by a [[committee]] of the votes given at an election, for the purpose of correcting the [[poll]].
#: {{rfquotek|en|Brande & C}}

